OpenAI is recruiting local helpers to sit down with small business owners and work through real AI tasks with them. The program is called OpenWorks. For an eligible business, OpenAI's pages say taking part is free. For the people doing the helping, it is a paid contract role. This explainer covers both sides, the limits, and what we could not find out. Details are as read on 27 November 2026.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. OpenAI competes with Anthropic. Stackbrook did not test the program, has not applied to it and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.*

## What a business gets

OpenAI says it is building a nationwide network of OpenWorks Partners, and the program page says it is recruiting its first ones. For businesses, the page lists three things:

- one-on-one support from a partner;
- practical exercises with AI using tools the business already works with; and
- three months of ChatGPT Business at no cost.

Owners do not need technical experience. The help article also lists no set time commitment for business owners, which is not the same as saying none will be needed. Plan to bring a specific task and some time to practise.

The word "free" applies to participation in the pilot and the included three months. The pages we read do not say what happens when the three months end, whether a paid plan starts automatically, or how many user seats are included. We could not read OpenAI's ChatGPT Business billing help pages, so we do not report a price. Ask about all three points before your team starts relying on the account.

## Who qualifies

The program page describes eligible applicants as independently owned US small businesses, and names restaurants, retailers, service firms and manufacturers as examples. We did not find a definition of "small" by headcount or revenue.

Three groups are excluded: software and SaaS companies, venture-backed businesses, and businesses owned by private equity. The help article says nonprofits and faith-based organisations may apply, and should choose the closest industry on the form.

## Where the pilot runs

The program page says the pilot is now live in the communities it shows, and its list has 20. The chart below shows them.

![Grid of 20 tiles naming the OpenWorks pilot communities, from Chicago and Kansas City to Seattle and New York](/images/site1/openai-openworks-free-ai-help-small-businesses-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. Tiles follow the order on the OpenWorks page and are not placed geographically.*

Being outside these places does not stop you sending a form, but the help article says there is no guarantee the program will launch in an applicant's area. It also notes that the program page does not list an international waitlist or a way to propose a new location.

## How to apply

There are three forms: one to get help for a business, one to apply as a partner and one to nominate someone. A nomination needs a location and a reason, and the nominator does not have to make an introduction.

A few practical points from the help article:

- **Data questions.** The business form asks which kinds of data are involved, such as payment card or banking details, health data covered by the US HIPAA law, or personal records. Choose categories only. Do not paste in anyone's actual records.
- **Confirmation.** The on-screen message is your confirmation. An email follows only if the team decides to move forward. The confirmation screen may say "nomination" even after a partner application.
- **No edits.** The help article says there is no published way to update an application yet. Save a copy of your answers and do not resubmit the form.
- **Follow-up.** The article gives openworks@openai.com for questions about updates but says that inbox is not open yet. If you have already contacted OpenAI Support, it says to reply in that same conversation, adding the form link and the date you submitted if you have not already.

The help article says the partner form lists no application deadline, and we found none for businesses.

## The partner side

Partners are contractors. According to the help article, they commit at least four full days a month plus one monthly workshop. The program page advertises a monthly stipend of up to $8,000, and also mentions a community, tools, resources and workshop support.

"Up to" matters. The help article says the program page does not explain how the amount is calculated or how long it is paid. The pages we read also do not mention expenses or tax treatment, so these are open questions to raise with OpenAI.

Individuals and small teams with practical AI experience can apply, and the form lists no degree requirement. The application asks for one problem you solved with AI, what happened, and a social or portfolio link. Applicants without a registered company can enter "N/A" in the company field. Software and AI consultancies are encouraged to apply as partners. Asked about remote work or work from outside the US, the help article answers "Not yet" and says that for this pilot OpenAI prefers partners who work in person with US small businesses.

## What the examples do and do not show

OpenAI's page includes short stories about businesses it presents as having worked with OpenWorks Partners. At Sign Badgers in Fargo, pricing checks are said to have gone from hours to 15 to 20 minutes, and a staff member says the improvements save her two to five hours a week. The owner of a Charleston business, Ghost Grow 843, estimates his response rate rose from 20% to 75%, and the page says he gained 10 new restaurant customers within four months. These are results published by the company running the program. We did not verify them, and they are not a forecast for your business.

## A separate SBDC effort

A separate OpenAI announcement dated 30 September 2026 describes a partnership with America's SBDC, a network of small business development centres. It said it plans to train about 150 centre advisers in an initial phase, with a target of reaching at least 1,000 small businesses through in-person training. That page does not state costs or how owners sign up, so ask your local centre whether it is taking part. The announcement does not mention OpenWorks, and we found nothing there saying it covers areas outside the pilot.

## Before you say yes

- Pick one repeated task, such as quoting, stock lists or customer replies, and note how long it takes today. That gives you something to compare against.
- Ask who can see the business information you share, both the partner and the software account. The pages we read do not set out these data terms.
- Ask what happens to files, chats and any custom tools after the three months, and what continuing would cost.
- Check AI output before it reaches a customer, especially prices and promises.

## What we could not confirm

We did not find a launch date on the program page, a length for the pilot, how businesses or partners are selected, or how long a decision takes. We also did not find terms for data handling or for the end of the free period. These pages can change, so check the current version before applying.
